      <div class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Checkpoints</dt>
          <dd>A commit paired with the session that produced it. Created automatically on commit; identified by a 26-character ULID; stored as an independent git ref.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Graph</dt>
          <dd>Precomputed code map via tree-sitter. Commands: <code>query</code> (ranked search from a description), <code>def</code>, <code>neighbors</code>, <code>impact</code>, <code>diff</code> (semantic diff between revisions), <code>snapshot</code> (NDJSON export), <code>health</code>. Reads uncommitted edits by default; <code>--head</code> restricts to the committed tree. Scored 94.74% on LoCoMo with zero index-time tokens, against mem0's 50.85M.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Brain</dt>
          <dd>Repo memory. <code>entire brain setup</code> indexes the repo, mines facts from up to 25 past sessions in the background, and installs a watcher. Facts are categorised into <code>architecture</code>, <code>constraints</code>, <code>preferences</code>, <code>project</code>, <code>workflow</code>; <code>--global</code> facts apply across repos and are labelled <code>(global)</code> on recall. Document extraction is deterministic and local — no model, no network, no OCR.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Agentic search</dt>
          <dd>One query across repos, commits and sessions — semantic and literal. Available in the CLI, on entire.io, and from your coding agent.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Labs</dt>
          <dd>Experimental and very demo-friendly: <code>entire review</code> (send a branch to several agents for an intent-aware review), <code>entire blame</code>, <code>entire why</code> (why does this line exist), <code>entire experts</code>, <code>entire tokens</code>, <code>entire dispatch</code>, <code>entire recap</code>.</dd>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-title">Entire · the ten minutes that matter</div>
        <pre><span class="cm"># 1 — install (macOS/Linux; see docs for scoop/powershell/go)</span>
brew install --cask entireio/tap/entire
curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash   <span class="cm"># alternative</span>

<span class="cm"># 2 — turn it on in the repo, naming your agent</span>
cd ~/Desktop/travel_agent
entire login
entire enable --agent claude-code      <span class="cm"># or codex, cursor, copilot-cli, droid, opencode, pi, antigravity</span>
entire status

<span class="cm"># 3 — work normally. the commit is what creates the checkpoint.</span>
git add agent/planner.py
git commit -m "Add multi-city flight search with retry budget"

<span class="cm"># 4 — look at what it captured (this is your submission evidence)</span>
entire checkpoint list
entire checkpoint explain HEAD
entire search "why did we cap the retry budget at three"

<span class="cm"># 5 — hand your coding agent the map and the memory</span>
entire plugin install graph
entire graph init-agents --repo .            <span class="cm"># writes guidance into AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md</span>
entire graph query --profile full "flight search ranking"
entire graph impact --symbol rank_itineraries

entire brain setup
entire brain init-agents
entire brain remember "Flight prices are cached 15 min; never re-query inside one plan_trip run"
entire brain query "how do we handle provider timeouts"
entire brain mcp --print-config              <span class="cm"># wire Brain into Claude Code as an MCP server</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p class="note">Settings live in <code>.entire/settings.json</code> (team-shared, committed) and <code>.entire/settings.local.json</code> (personal, gitignored). Agent hooks are written to each agent's own config — <code>.claude/settings.json</code>, <code>.codex/hooks.json</code>, <code>.cursor/hooks.json</code>, <code>.github/hooks/entire.json</code>, <code>.factory/settings.json</code>, <code>.opencode/plugins/entire.ts</code>, <code>.pi/extensions/entire/index.ts</code>, <code>.agents/hooks.json</code>. If capture looks wrong, <code>entire doctor</code> diagnoses it. For the GitHub-App path your org admin has to install the app, and access then follows existing GitHub collaborator permissions.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= NEATLOGS ================= -->
    <section>
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="swatch" style="background:var(--neat)"></span>Tool two</div>
        <h2>Neatlogs</h2>
        <p class="tagline">“The fastest path from failure to fix” · Python + TypeScript SDKs · MIT · <span class="dmono">ingest.neatlogs.com</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p>Neatlogs is the host, and it is the tool your score depends on most directly, because it is the only one that produces evidence about how well your agent <em>works</em>. Its positioning is a deliberate jab at plain tracing: collecting traces is the easy part, and a human reading a 400-span tree to find the one bad tool call is the expensive part. So the product is arranged as a pipeline that ends at a patch, not at a chart.</p>
        <p>Instrumentation is genuinely one line, because the SDK monkey-patches provider libraries at import time. That detail has one consequence worth burning into your memory: <strong><code>neatlogs.init()</code> must run before you import <code>openai</code>, <code>anthropic</code>, <code>langgraph</code> or anything else you want traced.</strong> Get the order wrong and you get a working agent with a silently empty dashboard — which is a documented failure mode, not a hypothetical one.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>The data model: a trace is a tree of typed spans</h3>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p>A trace is one complete agent run. Spans nest to mirror your real call hierarchy at runtime, and each span has a <em>kind</em> that tells the UI how to render and analyse it. Eligible roots are <code>WORKFLOW</code>, <code>CHAIN</code>, <code>AGENT</code>, <code>MCP_TOOL</code> or <code>LLM</code>; beneath them sit <code>TOOL</code>, <code>RETRIEVER</code> and <code>GUARDRAIL</code>. Here is what one <code>plan_trip</code> run looks like once it lands:</p>
      </div>

      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-title">One trace · travel_agent</div>
        <pre>WORKFLOW   plan_trip  BLR→LIS 2026-11-14  ·  12.4s  ·  $0.0412  ·  9 spans
│
├─ AGENT       itinerary_planner                            11.8s
│  ├─ LLM      claude-opus-5   2,141 in / 388 out            3.1s   first token 0.42s
│  ├─ TOOL     search_flights(BLR→LIS, 2026-11-14)           1.9s   ok · 23 results
│  ├─ TOOL     search_flights(BLR→LIS, 2026-11-15)           4.4s   <span class="hl">retry 2 · provider timeout</span>
│  ├─ RETRIEVER hotel_notes   8 chunks · top score 0.71      0.3s
│  └─ LLM      claude-opus-5   6,004 in / 912 out            2.0s   <span class="hl">◆ detection: PII in prompt</span>
│
└─ GUARDRAIL   budget_check                                  0.1s   <span class="hl">fail · ₹4,180 &gt; ₹3,000 cap</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p>Per span you get inputs, outputs, latency (including first-token latency on LLM spans), token counts and cache metrics, cost, trace and span IDs, plus whatever you attach via <code>span.set_attribute()</code>. In the dashboard you can filter by span kind, cost, latency, or by <em>votes</em> — a thumbs up/down on a span's output, which is how human judgement gets attached to specific steps. Session Replay plays the run back; Comments let a non-engineer attach customer context to the exact run that produced it.</p>
        <p class="note">Transport limits worth knowing before you log a whole PDF: text values are capped at 100,000 UTF-8 bytes and each trace has a 1 MB budget. Large media needs <code>uploads_enabled=True</code> explicitly.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>The loop that closes across both tools</h3>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 292" role="img" aria-label="A circuit. Top row left to right: an agent run in production, its span tree sent to ingest, and a detection firing. Down the right edge to the bottom row, read right to left: an investigation producing root cause and evidence, a fix handed to a coding agent, and a commit that Entire turns into a checkpoint. Up the left edge back to the agent run, where the next run verifies the detection stopped firing.">
